{%- comment -%}
  Normalised "finish family" key for a product: the part of its title that is
  identical across every finish of the same model, so two products can be
  compared to see whether they are the same machine in a different finish.

  When custom.combined_listing_family is populated, that explicit key is used.
  Otherwise, the snippet falls back to the title-normalisation rules below.

  Two things are dropped from the title, both read from the product's own
  metafields - nothing about any specific finish is written into this snippet:

    - the finish word, from custom.product_color ("Black", "Silver", ...)
    - the series code that carries the finish, taken as the first segment of
      custom.product_code ("FFB-DAP" -> "FFB", "FFS-DAP" -> "FFS")

  The series code has to come out too: those products are titled "French Fitness
  FFB Black <Type>" / "French Fitness FFS Silver <Type>", so removing only the
  colour word would leave FFB vs FFS behind and the two finishes of one machine
  would never match. Codes whose prefix is not in the title (FFM-PLISPL, and any
  other series that does not put its code in the title) are left untouched.

  Both values are only removed as whole space-delimited words, so a colour or
  code that happens to be part of a longer word is left alone.

  Accepts:
    product - {Object} the product to build the key for (required)
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign explicit_family = product.metafields.custom.combined_listing_family.value
  assign color = product.metafields.custom.product_color.value
  assign code = product.metafields.custom.product_code.value

  if explicit_family != blank
    assign key = explicit_family
  else
  assign key = ' ' | append: product.title | append: ' '

  if color != blank
    assign color_word = ' ' | append: color | append: ' '
    assign key = key | replace: color_word, ' '
  endif

  if code != blank
    assign series = code | split: '-' | first
    if series != blank
      assign series_word = ' ' | append: series | append: ' '
      assign key = key | replace: series_word, ' '
    endif
  endif
  endif

  echo key | strip | downcase | split: ' ' | join: ' '
-%}
